<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rus' people</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century–11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Rus', Russes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rus', also known as Russes, were a people of early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> whom the scholarly consensus holds to have been originally Norsemen, mainly from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, who settled and ruled along the river routes between the Baltic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> from around the 8th to 11th centuries. Their first centres were Ladoga, founded in the mid-8th century, and Rurikovo Gorodische, founded in the mid-9th, at either end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volkhov-river">Volkhov River</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> the ruling Norsemen gradually assimilated into the East Slavic population, and the elite lost Old Norse by the second half of the 11th century. They gave their name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Rus' is generally considered a borrowing from Finnic Ruotsi, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. It may come more directly from the Old Norse róðr, which referred to rowing and the fleet levy, or through Rōþin, an older name for the Swedish coastal region Roslagen; the two theories are not mutually exclusive. The two original centres lay at opposite ends of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volkhov-river">Volkhov River</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ilmen">Lake Ilmen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ladoga">Lake Ladoga</a>, a territory the Norsemen likely called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gardar-greenland">Gardar</a>. From there the name passed to the Middle Dnieper, and the Rus' moved east to the Finnic tribes and south to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The sources describe them differently. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a>, compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> at the start of the 13th century, records the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a> exacting tribute from Slavic and Finnic tribes in 859; historians agree it mainly reflects the politics of its own time. It also preserves the texts of Rus'–Byzantine treaties of 911, 945 and 971, whose Rus' signatories mostly had Norse names, though by 945 some had Slavic ones. The Annals of St. Bertin record that in 839 men who called themselves Rhos, who had come to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> at Ingelheim with a Byzantine delegation, turned out to be Swedes.</p><p>Byzantine writers called them Rhos and seem to have seen them as distinct from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>; two of them called them dromitai, a word suggesting movement by waterways. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> gave the names of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> cataracts in the language of the Rus' and of the Slavs. Arabic sources, with over 30 relevant passages, portray the Rus' as a raiding and trading diaspora or as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> under the Volga Bulghars or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>. The most extensive is by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-fadlan">Ahmad ibn Fadlan</a>, who visited Volga Bulgaria in 922.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Scandinavian influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> was strongest in the late 9th and the 10th centuries. In 976 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, where he gathered a force that he used to conquer Kievan Rus'. He converted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a> in 988, after which Norse influence declined considerably, and in the 11th century the Norse appear as Varangian mercenaries serving the princely family. The elite became bilingual around 950, and Old East Slavic can be shown to have become their native language only at the end of the 11th century.</p><p>The Rus' gave their name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and figure in the national histories of Russia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and Belarus. Because of this, a set of alternative &quot;anti-Normanist&quot; views exists, largely confined to a minor group of Eastern European scholars.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rus' people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
